+/* agent_screen.c - the board's status screen.
+ *
+ * A 390x450 panel (CO5300 AMOLED): a white status bar with the link dot and
+ * the uptime, the announcement in a big card, and five rows of live machine
+ * state underneath -- the command the user just gave, the PPP link, the model
+ * the board is routed to, the last announcement played from the image, and how
+ * many commands the router has served.
+ *
+ * White background and 34 px body text on purpose: this screen is filmed, and
+ * the first version (dark blue, 22 px) was unreadable on camera.  Everything
+ * shown is a real reading -- the link row comes from getifaddrs(), the model
+ * and the call count from the LLM router, the announcement row from the clip
+ * player.  Nothing on this screen is decorative.
+ *
+ * Bring-up mirrors apps/examples/lvgldemo: lv_init(), lv_nuttx_dsc_init()
+ * with the framebuffer and touch nodes, lv_nuttx_init(), then a task that
+ * pumps lv_timer_handler().  It runs in its own thread for two reasons: the
+ * LCD/touch nodes are registered by a deferred board task (so they may not
+ * exist when the agent boots), and LVGL is single-threaded, so the thread
+ * that initialises it is the one that must call into it.  Writers only touch
+ * plain strings under a mutex; every LVGL call happens in that one thread.
+ *
+ * Text is rendered with a FreeType CJK font subset shipped in the romfs
+ * (/etc/fonts/NotoSansSC-sub.ttf, GB2312 level 1+2).  LVGL's built-in
+ * lv_font_simsun_16_cjk is only a fallback: its glyph set is a thin,
+ * arbitrary subset and common characters came out as boxes.
+ */
+
